Edit {"replace_all":false,"file_path":"D:\Lean Book\automation\Final\Appendix_HistoryOfLean_lgriffin.md","old_string":"Henry Ford took Taylor's principles and married them to the moving assembly line. In 1913, Ford's Highland Park plant began producing the Model T on a continuously moving line where each worker performed a single, standardised operation as the car moved past. The results were staggering. Assembly time for a Model T chassis dropped from over twelve hours to ninety-three minutes. The price of the car fell from $850 to $260 over the life of the model. Ford proved that flow -- the continuous, uninterrupted movement of work through a sequence of stations -- could achieve extraordinary gains in both speed and cost.

But Ford's system had a structural limitation that would define its era and constrain its successors. It depended on scale and uniformity. The moving assembly line achieved its efficiency by producing enormous volumes of identical products. "Any customer can have a car painted any colour that he wants," Ford famously said, "so long as it is black." Variety was the enemy of flow. Changeovers between models were so expensive and disruptive that Ford resisted them for years, even as consumer tastes shifted and competitors offered choice.","new_string":"Henry Ford took Taylor's principles and married them to the moving assembly line. Standardisation was the foundation: one product, one colour, one process. "Any customer can have a car painted any colour that he wants," Ford said, "so long as it is black." That wasn't stubbornness -- it was a design decision. By eliminating variation, Ford could optimise every station on the line for a single, repeatable operation. In 1913, his Highland Park plant began producing the Model T on a continuously moving line where each worker performed one standardised task as the car moved past. The results were staggering. Assembly time for a chassis dropped from over twelve hours to ninety-three minutes. The price of the car fell from $850 to $260 over the life of the model. Ford proved that flow -- the continuous, uninterrupted movement of work through a sequence of stations -- could achieve extraordinary gains in both speed and cost.

But Ford's system had a structural limitation that would define its era and constrain its successors. The very standardisation that made it powerful also made it brittle. The moving assembly line achieved its efficiency by producing enormous volumes of identical products. Variety was the enemy of flow. Changeovers between models were so expensive and disruptive that Ford resisted them for years, even as consumer tastes shifted and competitors offered choice."}
